Myth: When the golden wheat of the first harvest was gathered, the spirits realized the cycle was not a static destination but a living breath. Elara, having cast her seeds, waited in the silence of the void, her hands empty but her heart anchored in the faith of the return. Aster, the keeper of the abundance, knew that the harvest was not his alone to consume. To hoard the grain would be to choke the soil, turning the field back into the barren dust of the past.

Instead, Aster chose the path of the bridge. He looked at the fifteen stalks, acknowledging the five that had birthed them. He understood that a return was not a loss of treasure, but a vital investment in the continuation of the garden. He handed back a portion, a gesture that acted as a new seed for Elara to carry forward. This act solidified the unseen bond between Sower and Keeper. They saw that the true value of their meeting was the trust itself, a fountain that never ran dry so long as each honored the other's gamble. Their dance of giving and returning became the rhythm of the universe, proving that cooperation is the only true alchemy.

